<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceuta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceuta is a port city on the North African coast opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. Its conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">King João I</a> of Portugal and his sons, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, in 1415 initiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s overseas expansion. The city proved a strategic disappointment, as Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> were redirected to bypass it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceuta.jpg" alt="Ceuta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hansi_2010 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_militar_del_Desnarigado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ceuta lies on the North African coast, 17 kilometres across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> from the Spanish mainland, and shares a land border of 6.4 kilometres with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. It has an area of 18.5 square kilometres. Monte Hacho, on the La Almina Peninsula above the port, is one of the possible sites of the southern Pillar of Hercules of Greek legend; the other is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Jebel Musa</a>. The Strait of Gibraltar is an important military and commercial chokepoint, and the narrow isthmus joining the La Almina Peninsula to the African mainland made the site easy to defend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> established an outpost there early in the 1st millennium BCE, which continued under Roman rule as Septem Fratres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> crossed the strait around 425 CE, but lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> and Septem in a series of Berber revolts. Around 533 the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> made Septem the westernmost outpost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and the empire, which did not push far inland, made it their regional capital in place of Tingis. It was annexed by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber Revolt</a>, and rebuilt in the ninth century. For much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> powers north and south of the strait vied for control of it as a key port in the so-called Battle of the Strait.</p><p>On the morning of 21 August 1415 King John I of Portugal led his sons and their forces in a surprise assault known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-ceuta">Conquest of Ceuta</a>. About 45,000 Portuguese arrived on 200 ships, caught the defenders off guard and took the town by nightfall, with only eight casualties. After the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian of Portugal</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578 and a succession war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> was crowned Philip I of Portugal in April 1581, and Ceuta passed to the Hispanic Monarchy. On 1 January 1668 King Afonso VI of Portugal ceded it to King Carlos II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><p>Moroccan forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a> besieged the city from 1694 to 1727, the longest siege in history, and the city lost its Portuguese character. It was besieged unsuccessfully again in 1790 and 1791, and British forces occupied it from 1810 to 1815. A dispute over the border led to the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> of 1859 to 1860, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wad-ras">Treaty of Wad Ras</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ceuta is an autonomous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a status it gained through its Statute of Autonomy in March 1995; before then it was a municipality of the province of Cádiz. Spanish is its official language, and Moroccan Arabic is also widely spoken. Its population is mainly Spanish Christians and Arab Muslims, with small Sephardic Jewish and Sindhi Hindu minorities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ceuta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To check the Fatimids, Abd al-Rahman occupied Melilla in 927 and <b>Ceuta</b> in 931 and built a network of client Berber chiefs in northern Morocco, giving the caliphate a foothold in North Africa for most of its existence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France allowed the sultan back in 1955, and in March 1956 Morocco regained its independence as the Kingdom of Morocco; Spain gave up its northern protectorate a month later but kept <b>Ceuta</b> and Melilla, which Morocco claims still.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans sought control of the commercial traffic of the Strait of Gibraltar from Algeciras, which the Nasrids of Granada had ceded to them, with shipyards at Salé and <b>Ceuta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese set up small trading posts on the lower Congo and a more important settlement at Soyo, and from the start their expeditions aimed at the conversion of the peoples they met, as they had since the conquest of <b>Ceuta</b> in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of the Muslim port of <b>Ceuta</b> on the North African coast on 21 August 1415, led by King João I and his sons, including Prince Henry, later celebrated as “the Navigator”, began the imperial project, though Ceuta itself proved a commercial disappointment as Saharan caravan trade…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the 570s, retook Córdoba, pacified the north without wholly conquering it and in 585 destroyed the Suebic kingdom in a civil war after King Miro's death; Sisebut took Málaga, and Swinthila captured all the Byzantine holdings by 625 while a foothold was gained at <b>Ceuta</b> in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
